arly, but B has risk of loss, so if we define 'unfavorable' as negative or below expected value, B has 50% chance of -3000 (unfavorable) and 50% of 3000 (favorable). A has 100% of 1500 (favorable). So B assigns 50% to unfavorable, A assigns 0%. So B is more right-skewed than A. Therefore, the reason would prefer B. But this contradicts the intuitive reading, because 'right-skewed' in common statistical terms means a long right tail (high upside, low probability), but the reason defines it inversely: 'greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes' — so this is actually *left-skew* in standard terms. So the reason uses 'right-skewed' to mean what is typically left-skewed or negatively skewed. Thus, the reason prefers the lottery with more downside risk concentration. In this case, B has 50% downside, A has 0%, so B is more 'right-skewed' per this definition, and the reason would prefer B. So the reason can be applied, and it would prefer B.",
